\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that crossing rate reverses the central finding: at biased layers, the HS advantage appears scale-dependent; at corrected layers, all four models are significant$^\dagger$ (\cref{sec:evaluation}). The mechanism is representation compression at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), which inflates crossing rate independently of discriminative power. We hypothesize the effect generalizes beyond safety probing to any threshold-based detection study. The HS advantage is robust across BA-optimal (mid-depth) and precision-optimal (deep) layers; it is the crossing-rate bias toward shallow layers that masks the effect (\cref{tab:ba_layer_comparison}: all 16 model-metric comparisons pass HB at BA-selected layers, precision +13.7 to +25.4pp). This strengthens our recommendation: any threshold-independent layer selection criterion recovers the HS advantage.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Both temporal context and modality choice improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale: at 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}); at 32B, relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) provides consistent improvement regardless of modality, while modality choice requires principled layer selection.

\paragraph{Encoder-conditional advantage.}
Part of the observed advantage may reflect text encoder quality. At matched 384d via PCA (79--92\% variance retained), the precision advantage persists on all four models$^\dagger$ (+16.8 to +23.1pp, HB corrected), eliminating capacity gap as the sole explanation. A stronger 1024d encoder closes most of the significance gap (1/4 HB-significant), suggesting a continuum rather than a binary modality distinction. LLM-based text encoders (e.g., E5-Mistral-7B, 4096d; \citealp{wang2024e5mistral}) would likely further narrow the gap, bounding the practical lifetime of any HS advantage claim.

\paragraph{Per-model evidence and design implications.}
Robustness splits by model, not scale: OT-7B and QwQ-32B (5/5 seeds HB each) are most stable, R1-8B FPR is seed-fragile (3/5 HB), and R1-32B passes all seeds but its advantage is position-mediated (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}). Distillation and native reasoning training produce qualitatively different safety representation geometry within the same architecture. PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, and position residualization (3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage) provide converging evidence of genuine safety representations; direct erasure methods (LEACE; \citealp{belrose2023leace}) remain untested.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Safety in Reasoning Models.}
Reasoning models introduce a qualitatively new failure mode: the model's own chain of thought can transition from safety-aware deliberation to harmful generation without adversarial input modification~\cite{wang2025lrmsafety}.

\textit{Attack characterization.} Self-jailbreak has been documented with step-level annotations~\cite{harmthoughts}, with longer chains increasing circumvention probability~\cite{selfjailbreak}. Reasoning models can autonomously jailbreak other LLMs with 97\% success~\cite{hagendorff2026lrm_jailbreak}. H-CoT~\cite{hcot2025} shows reasoning chains can be externally hijacked. Unfaithful reasoning~\cite{chen2025unfaithful} further motivates hidden-state monitoring that bypasses text. Safety-aligned models maintain refusal intentions during thinking but experience a sharp drop at output generation~\cite{yin2025refusal_cliff}, a phenomenon our temporal probes target. Refusal dynamics in standard LLMs~\cite{hu2026refusal_dynamics} and information leakage in traces~\cite{ahrend2026safer_traces} provide additional context.

\textit{Detection.} CoT monitoring can detect reward hacking but optimization pressure promotes obfuscation~\cite{baker2025monitoring}. CoT monitorability is valuable but fragile~\cite{korbak2025cot_monitorability}. GuardReasoner~\cite{xiang2025guardreasoner} trains a dedicated reasoning-based guard model.

\textit{Mitigation.} SafeThink~\cite{safethink} steers early reasoning toward safety, SafeRemind~\cite{saferemind} inserts entropy-triggered reminders, and Chain-of-Guardrails~\cite{mao2025selfjailbreak_reasoning} proposes guardrail insertion during reasoning. None of these conducts a systematic modality comparison for commitment detection.

\paragraph{Hidden-State Safety Detection.}
Internal activations can detect safety violations in standard LLMs. \citet{zhou2024alignment} show safety-relevant information distributes across layers, while \citet{qi2025shallow_alignment} find that alignment operates primarily over early output tokens. \citet{arditi2024refusal} identify a single refusal direction, but \citet{zhao2025harmfulness} show harmfulness and refusal are encoded separately. \citet{joad2026refusal_multi} reveal eleven category-specific refusal directions, and \citet{wollschlager2025geometry} formalize multi-dimensionality through concept cones, consistent with our finding that single-direction methods are insufficient. TrajGuard~\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance from safe trajectories; SafeSwitch~\cite{safeswitch} trains hidden-state classifiers; SIREN~\cite{siren} monitors internal representations during generation; SafeDream~\cite{safedream2026} proposes proactive world models for detection. These methods assume threats from external input manipulation and operate at the token level on instruction-tuned models. The self-jailbreak setting differs because transitions are internal and span multiple reasoning steps. \citet{kadali2025internal} decompose internal layer representations to separate jailbreak from benign prompts; their results confirm that layer-wise structure carries safety-relevant signal. We reproduce TrajGuard (\cref{sec:negative}) and find its geometric approach fails because discriminative signal resides in minor PCA components.

\paragraph{Probing Methodology and Layer Selection.}
Causal abstraction~\cite{geiger2025causal} provides a theoretical foundation for mechanistic interpretability by formalizing when high-level causal models faithfully describe neural computation, grounding the use of internal representations for behavioral prediction. Linear probing has a long history from layer-wise classifiers~\cite{alain2017understanding} through sentence probing~\cite{conneau2018probing} to comprehensive surveys~\cite{belinkov2019analysis,belinkov2022probing,rogers2020primer}. Layer-wise analyses reveal hierarchical linguistic structure across depth~\cite{jawahar2019bert,liu2019linguistic}, and information-theoretic formulations address probe complexity~\cite{voita2020information,pimentel2020information}. \citet{hewitt2019control} introduce control tasks to distinguish structure from memorization, which we adopt (\cref{app:control}). \citet{ravfogel2020null} introduce iterative nullspace projection (INLP) for concept removal; \citet{ravfogel2022rlace} extend this to closed-form linear adversarial concept erasure. \citet{belrose2023leace} establish theoretical limits on linear probe extraction; \citet{elazar2021amnesic} test whether probed information is causally used. \citet{tenney2019bert} show syntactic information in middle layers and semantics in deeper layers. \citet{zhang2025probing_selfverify} apply hidden-state probing to self-verification in reasoning models, a complementary perspective on what reasoning-model representations encode. Concurrent work by \citet{alvarez2026transport_geometry} uses hidden-state geometry for step-level hallucination detection; the approach shares our temporal probing framework but targets a different phenomenon. \citet{boxo2025linear_probes} show that linear probes can rely on textual evidence rather than deeper representations, which motivates our comparison of hidden-state probes against text-based monitors. Despite this foundation, layer selection remains underexamined: most studies select by a single metric without sensitivity analysis. We demonstrate that this practice can reverse a study's central conclusion (\cref{sec:layer-results}).

\paragraph{Change-Point Detection.}
BOCPD~\cite{adams2007bayesian} detects abrupt distributional shifts; CCS~\cite{burns2023discovering} discovers latent truth directions via consistency constraints; RepE~\cite{zou2023representation} identifies concept directions through mean differences. We adapt all three to temporal commitment detection (\cref{sec:negative}). BOCPD and CUSUM fail because commitment is gradual. CCS provides no gain because its consistency objective misaligns with temporal structure. RepE shows inconsistent performance across models (0.756 on QwQ, 0.559 on R1-32B). These failures motivate supervised probes with principled layer selection as the minimal effective methodology.
